TI  - Liver Transplantation for Treatment of Intra- and Extrahepatic Cholangiocarcinomas
T2  - VISZERALMEDIZIN
AB  - Liver Transplantation for Treatment of Intra- and Extrahepatic Cholangiocarcinomas The treatment of intra- and extrahepatic cholangiocarcinomas remains a medical challenge. Due to poor efficiency of conventional chemotherapy surgical treatment modalities represent the only chance for long-term survival and cure in patients suffering from these tumor entities. The introduction of new procedures, in particular extended liver resections, lead to an increasing number of curatively treated patients as well as significant improvements of the long-term results after curative resection. However, due to local tumor extension, intrahepatic metastasis, infiltration of main vascular and biliary structures and insufficient remnant liver function, many patients are not suitable for radical resections. Liver transplantation is a curative treatment option for irresectable tumors and is therefore the only chance to achieve long-term survival and cure in numerous patients. However, due to organ shortage and the resulting necessity to allocate the available organs to patients with the best prognosis, cholangiocarcinomas currently do not represent a general indication for liver transplantation. In recent years the results after transplantation for the different entities of cholangiocarcinomas could be improved by application of new treatment protocols. The most promising long-term results were achieved by a protocol using neoadjuvant chemoradiation in hilar cholangiocarcinoma, with long-term survival rates not inferior to results after transplantation for benign liver diseases or early stages of hepatocellular carcinoma. In recent years, improving long-term results after transplantation for intra- and extrahepatic cholangiocarcinomas were also reported from other centers, which lead to increasing attention for liver transplantation in the treatment concept of these tumor entities. The establishment of suitable selection criteria, which would allow identifying patients with the best prognosis, will be an important tool to further improve the long-term results after liver transplantation in patients with intra- and extrahepatic cholangiocarcinomas.
