TI  - Cholangiocarcinoma
T2  - GI CANCER
AB  - Cholangiocarcinoma or Klatskin's tumor has always been a challenging management problem, both for cure and palliation through recent results are more encouraging. Reduction of the operative mortality rate could be attributed to the use of vigorous preoperative percutaneous biliary drainage and adequate rehydration. Inclusive of a caudate lobectomy had been accepted as an integal part of a curative hepatectomy, especially for lesions involving the confluence. Although the best operative procedure for palliation remains uncertain, transtumoral intubation and segment III cholangio-enteric bypass are popular. Retrospective data suggest that non-operative measures are equally effective as surgery for palliation and should be considered in patients with grossly unresectable disease. The use of self-expandable metallic stents did not solve all the problems associated with the conventional endoprosthesis. Further clinical data are required before the best choice of palliation could be decided.
